## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical understanding of lightweight, policy-driven OTCA telemetry for multi-tenant apps, emphasizing real-time decisioning, regulatory retention, and mobility across regions.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant residency and retention controls
- Ingestion-time redaction and field dropping
- Lightweight per-tenant policy engine with dynamic updates
- Edge-to-streaming architecture: edge gateway, regional Kafka, Parquet on S3 with tenant/date partitions
- Privacy, QoS, and policy-change verification

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo policy evaluator
function evaluate(event, policy) {
  if (policy.drop) return null;
  if (policy.redactUserId) event.user_id_hash = null;
  return event;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test policy updates without downtime?
- How would you monitor for privacy violations in production?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical schema evolution, privacy at ingest, and how it affects dashboards.

## Key Concepts
- OTCA streaming, schema evolution, privacy policies, per-tenant isolation
- Schema Registry, Avro/JSON schemas, backward compatibility
- Ingestion pipeline and storage layout (Parquet partitions)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo policy application at ingest
function applyPolicy(tenant, payload){
  const policy = getPolicy(tenant);
  if(policy.redact) redact(payload, policy.fields);
  if(policy.drop) dropFields(payload, policy.fields);
  return payload;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you migrate existing data when a policy changes?
- How would you monitor schema compatibility over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of data governance in OTCA, practical ingestion-time policy enforcement, and real-world routing/partitioning decisions for privacy.

## Key Concepts
- Data classification and ingestion-time policy
- Per-tenant privacy controls and lineage
- Streaming routing to separate topics
- Parquet partitioning and audit trails

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple policy lookup
function decideAction(tenantId, category) {
  // naive rule: sensitive_financial always redact unless tenant whitelisted
  const policies = { 'sensitive_financial': { redact: true, whitelist: [] } };
  const c = policies[category];
  return c && c.redact ? 'redact' : 'allow';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the policy engine's behavior across tenants with differing policies?
- How would you implement schema evolution for payload while ensuring redaction doesn't break downstream consumers?
